Hmm, the test passes. Let me think about this more carefully. The issue says "Stringifying a parsed path that has a parameter name followed by text starting with an astral Unicode character that is an identifier continuation incorrectly fails to quote the parameter name."

The `ID_CONTINUE` regex is `/^[$\u200c\u200d\p{ID_Continue}]$/u`. And in `stringifyName`:

```javascript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The issue is with `next.value[0]` - when the text starts with an astral character (like `𐐀` which is U+10400), `next.value[0]` gives only the high surrogate (the first UTF-16 code unit), not the full code point.

Wait, but the test is passing. Let me think more carefully...

When `next.value` starts with `𐐀` (U+10400), `next.value[0]` returns the high surrogate `\uD800`. The `ID_CONTINUE` regex is `/^[$\u200c\u200d\p{ID_Continue}]$/u` which should NOT match a lone surrogate. So the name shouldn't be quoted... but the test says it should.

Wait, my test is checking that the round-trip works. Let me think about what the round-trip failure looks like:

1. Parse `/:a𐐀` - this creates tokens `[{type: "param", name: "a"}, {type: "text", value: "𐐀"}]`
2. Stringify the tokens - `stringifyName("a", {type: "text", value: "𐐀"})` is called. `ID.test("a")` is true.Since `next.value[0]` is the high surrogate, it won't match `ID_CONTINUE`, so the name isn't quoted and we get `:a𐐀`. When we parse that back, `:a` is the param, and `𐐀` should be the text. The`𐐀` character's first UTF-16 code unit is `\uD800`, which has the high bit set in a way that won't match `ID_START`. Let me reconsider what happens when parsing this string character by character.

When we spread `":a𐐀"` into individual code points, we get `[':', 'a', '𐐀']`. After consuming the colon and building up the name `a`, we check if the next character `𐐀` passes `ID_START`. The regex `/^[$_\p{ID_Start}]$/u` should match `𐐀` since it's a valid identifier start character in Unicode, so the name continues to accumulate it, giving us`a𐐀`. This means the second parse produces a different result than the first, breaking the round-trip.

The solution is to use `codePointAt(0)` instead of `next.value[0]` when checking if the next token starts with an ID_Continue character. I should verify my test actually demonstrates this failure case.

